assert check_k_elements([(4, 4), (4, 4, 4), (4, 4), (4, 4, 4, 4), (4, )], 4) == True
assert check_k_elements([(7, 7, 7), (7, 7)], 7) == True
assert check_k_elements([(9, 9), (9, 9, 9, 9)], 7) == False
[BEGIN]

answer:  def check_k_elements(tuple_list, k):
    all_elements = set()
    for tup in tuple_list:
        all_elements.update(tup)
    return len(all_elements) == k

# Test cases
assert check_k_elements([(4, 4), (4, 4, 4), (4, 4), (4, 4, 4, 4), (4, )], 4) == True
assert check_k_elements([(7, 7, 7), (7, 7)], 7) == True
assert check_k_elements([(9, 9), (9, 9, 9, 9)], 7) == False

This function `check_k_elements` takes a list of tuples and an integer `k` as input. It initializes an empty set `all_elements` and iterates through each tuple in the list, updating the set with the elements of each tuple. Finally, it checks if the length of the set is equal to `k`. If it is, it returns `True`; otherwise, it returns `False`.

The test cases provided at the end of the code should pass the given tests.
