You are an expert Python programmer, and here is your task: Write a function to find the list of lists with maximum length. Your code should pass these tests:

assert max_length([[0], [1, 3], [5, 7], [9, 11], [13, 15, 17]])==(3, [13, 15, 17])
assert max_length([[1], [5, 7], [10, 12, 14,15]])==(4, [10, 12, 14,15])
assert max_length([[5], [15,20,25]])==(3, [15,20,25])
[BEGIN]

answer:  def max_length(lst):
    max_list = None
    max_length = 0
    for sublist in lst:
        if len(sublist) > max_length:
            max_length = len(sublist)
            max_list = sublist
    return max_length, max_list

# Tests
assert max_length([[0], [1, 3], [5, 7], [9, 11], [13, 15, 17]]) == (3, [13, 15, 17])
assert max_length([[1], [5, 7], [10, 12, 14,15]]) == (4, [10, 12, 14,15])
assert max_length([[5], [15,20,25]]) == (3, [15,20,25])

This function `max_length` takes a list of lists as input and returns a tuple containing the length of the list with the maximum length and the list itself. The function iterates through each sublist in the list and keeps track of the one with the maximum length.
