You are an expert Python programmer, and here is your task: Write a function to perform the mathematical bitwise xor operation across the given tuples. Your code should pass these tests:

assert bitwise_xor((10, 4, 6, 9), (5, 2, 3, 3)) == (15, 6, 5, 10)
assert bitwise_xor((11, 5, 7, 10), (6, 3, 4, 4)) == (13, 6, 3, 14)
assert bitwise_xor((12, 6, 8, 11), (7, 4, 5, 6)) == (11, 2, 13, 13)
[BEGIN]

answer:  def bitwise_xor(tup1, tup2):
    result = []
    for i in range(len(tup1)):
        result.append(tup1[i] ^ tup2[i])
    return result

# Test cases
assert bitwise_xor((10, 4, 6, 9), (5, 2, 3, 3)) == (15, 6, 5, 10)
assert bitwise_xor((11, 5, 7, 10), (6, 3, 4, 4)) == (13, 6, 3, 14)
assert bitwise_xor((12, 6, 8, 11), (7, 4, 5, 6)) == (11, 2, 13, 13)

print("All tests passed!")

This code defines a function `bitwise_xor` that takes two tuples as input and returns a new tuple containing the bitwise XOR of the corresponding elements from the input tuples. The test cases provided are used to ensure the function works correctly.
